#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "8787186e-94bd-53da-9304-b0d3efbcb966" :question/math-academy-id "e-5823" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2$, $a$, and $2a^2$, so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}} + \\frac{1}{a} &= \\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}} \\\\\n6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}} + \\frac{1}{a}) &= 6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}}) + 6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) &= 6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}}) + 6a \\cdot a \\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) &= 3 \\cdot 2a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}}) + 6a \\cdot a \\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) &= 3 \\cdot 2a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n2 \\cdot (2a - 5) + 6a \\cdot (1) &= 3 \\cdot (a - 1) \\\\\n2(2a - 5) + 6a &= 3(a - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a$, and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2a - 5) + 6a &= 3(a - 1) \\\\\n4a - 10 + 6a &= 3a - 3 \\\\\n10a - 10 &= 3a - 3 \\\\\n10a - 3a &= -3 + 10 \\\\\n7a &= 7 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0$, we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "84d3492d-12bb-50c2-b5b4-5f08567eb4f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5825" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x} = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x$, so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 - \\frac{10x + 7}{8x} &= \\frac{6x - 3}{2x} \\\\\n8x \\cdot (3 - \\frac{10x + 7}{8x}) &= 8x \\cdot (\\frac{6x - 3}{2x}) \\\\\n8x \\cdot 3 - 8x \\cdot (\\frac{10x + 7}{8x}) &= 8x \\cdot (\\frac{6x - 3}{2x}) \\\\\n24x - 8x \\cdot (\\frac{10x + 7}{8x}) &= 4 \\cdot 2x \\cdot (\\frac{6x - 3}{2x}) \\\\\n24x - (10x + 7) &= 4 \\cdot (6x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x$, and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24x - (10x + 7) &= 4 \\cdot (6x - 3) \\\\\n14x - 7 &= 24x - 12 \\\\\n24x - 14x &= -7 + 12 \\\\\n10x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac 12\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "8e0fa2cd-2b29-510a-868f-4779de8536e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6260" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{x+2}{x} = \\dfrac{x-4}{x} + 2$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x$, so the least common denominator is $x$.\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x + 2}{x} &= \\frac{x - 4}{x} + 2 \\\\\nx \\cdot (\\frac{x + 2}{x}) &= x \\cdot (\\frac{x - 4}{x} + 2) \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{x + 2}{x} &= x \\cdot \\frac{x - 4}{x} + x \\cdot 2 \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{x + 2}{x} &= x \\cdot \\frac{x - 4}{x} + 2x \\\\\nx + 2 &= x - 4 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x$, and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2 &= x - 4 + 2x \\\\\nx + 2 &= 3x - 4 \\\\\n3x - x &= 2 + 4 \\\\\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0$, we accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "5d20e960-7554-5a05-b1e0-2b98b944e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-6261" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{1+x}{x} + \\dfrac{5+x}{6x} = \\dfrac{1-x}{3x}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x$, and $3x$, so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1 + x}{x} + \\frac{5 + x}{6x} &= \\frac{1 - x}{3x} \\\\\n6x \\cdot (\\frac{1 + x}{x} + \\frac{5 + x}{6x}) &= 6x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{3x} \\\\\n6x \\cdot (\\frac{1 + x}{x}) + 6x \\cdot (\\frac{5 + x}{6x}) &= 6x \\cdot (\\frac{1 - x}{3x}) \\\\\n6x \\cdot (\\frac{1 + x}{x}) + 6x \\cdot (\\frac{5 + x}{6x}) &= 2 \\cdot 3x \\cdot (\\frac{1 - x}{3x}) \\\\\n6x \\cdot (\\frac{1 + x}{x}) + 6x \\cdot (\\frac{5 + x}{6x}) &= 2 \\cdot 3x \\cdot (\\frac{1 - x}{3x}) \\\\\n6(1 + x) + (5 + x) &= 2(1 - x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x$, and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6(1 + x) + (5 + x) &= 2(1 - x) \\\\\n6 + 6x + 5 + x &= 2 - 2x \\\\\n7x + 11 &= 2 - 2x \\\\\n7x + 2x &= 2 - 11 \\\\\n9x &= -9 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831732b9-5a5b-5549-b9b9-e0f5f62705c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066744} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3666ff-5a6a-50da-9030-c1f343b16f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066745} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c2a5c9-8ffb-5be9-a404-4ad57b5037be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066746} {:answer/id #uuid "ec968105-093c-5b14-8a65-0e408a051d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066747} {:answer/id #uuid "34636b00-2ca9-5708-8c05-2636e0062611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec968105-093c-5b14-8a65-0e408a051d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066747} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24a5e2d-76c3-5609-ada6-aa3914d7266a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066743}] :question/id #uuid "058124fc-5b28-578d-8127-f08cc51043ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-3547" :question/problem "Solve for $b$ where $\\frac{5b + 8}{12b^{2}} =-\\frac{12}{36b} + \\frac{4b + 8}{12b^{2}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2118250-9306-56c2-a22d-5768bc4ffb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068184} {:answer/id #uuid "a9154e1f-ff2d-5801-a40c-65a99973105d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068185} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd488a7-a728-5ae7-a7e0-5a029c865574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068186} {:answer/id #uuid "03fba050-78d0-5807-a936-98513cd80a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068187} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8546b0-4fa4-527f-8463-21f57a46f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9154e1f-ff2d-5801-a40c-65a99973105d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068185} :answer-f